WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.660
- My worry is this.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.840
If I had, and I've said
this on "Bar Rescue",

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.100
there's 60 motorcycles in front of a bar,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.820
there's a bunch of people
that won't go to that bar.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.700
If there's a bunch of people
dressed in gang colors

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.030
in front of a bar, there's
people who won't go in that bar.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.520
Today, if there's a bunch
of people in front of a bar

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.413
that look irresponsible
as it relates to COVID,

00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:21.210
there are customers that
won't go in that bar.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.286
It's no different.

00:00:22.286 --> 00:00:24.953
(serious music)

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.270
- This is "The Rubin
Report" and I'm Dave Rubin.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.240
Reminder, everybody, to
subscribe to our YouTube channel

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.350
and tap that notification
bell so you see our new videos

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.390
in your feed.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.080
And joining me today is the host

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.680
and Executive Producer of "Bar Rescue",

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.890
the Chairman and CEO of Taffer Dynamics,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.840
and creator of "NFL Sunday",
I didn't know that one,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.270
Jon Taffer, welcome to "The Rubin Report".

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.890
- Good to be here, Dave.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.430
- I am really glad to
be talking to you today,

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.150
because as you know, we're kicking off

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.850
a small business week here,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.590
and I'm gonna be talking
to business owners all

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.630
over the country and I
think at least one in Canada

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.480
about how to reopen, how to
get business going again,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.290
how to get the economy going
again and everything else,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.250
and I thought you'd be a fun
guy to kick this off with.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.950
So first off, I can see you're sitting

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.180
in front of a bar right there.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.400
Where are ya?

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.840
What's going on in your
world at the moment

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.780
before we get into business?

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.050
Well, this is my, I'm in my home bar,

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.210
in my home in Las Vegas.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.370
So this is, which I've
turned into a studio lately.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.730
We have, our company
is based here in Vegas,

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.980
I've lived here in Vegas
for about eight years,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.130
and we moved all of our
equipment here to my house.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.240
So I've been working from home.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.260
I think I'm on my 93rd day at home

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.280
since we closed down production.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.530
Now, I'll go out on occasion.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.450
I've done one or two remotes
for some news channels.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.900
I've did a couple of Fox pieces,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.970
I did an MSNBC piece where we went out

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.210
on the Strip and did it.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.270
But I've maintained serious distances.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.260
And I'm one of the more cautious ones

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.060
in our society, I think.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.040
- Yeah, so I was reading a bit about that.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.990
So you are definitely on the
more cautious side of it.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.120
But I suspect as a guy like you,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.700
who you like getting out there
and going across the country

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.280
and talking to people, and
sometimes yelling at people

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.630
and getting pretty emotional with people.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.110
You must kinda miss that,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.480
that human interaction thing, right?

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.500
- Oh, big time.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.153
Matter of fact, Dave, and
I haven't told anyone this.

00:02:23.153 --> 00:02:24.850
I'll tell you this today.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.340
I have a 45-foot tour bus,

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.100
and I'm leaving next Tuesday
to go across the country

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.660
in my bus.
- Oh, wow.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.013
- And I'm doing it just to get out there.

00:02:34.013 --> 00:02:35.730
I can see some family along the way,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.980
but it's a safe way to travel these days.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.880
So I'll be out there and traveling

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.050
around the country for the
next three to five weeks.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.320
- So how did the whole
shut down affect production

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.153
for "Bar Rescue"?

00:02:49.153 --> 00:02:50.660
Were you guys producing right now,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.270
or were you in a break, or
are you coming back soon?

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.710
Where are you at?
- No,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.160
we were actually producing,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.680
and right in the middle
of our last episode.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.050
We had eight more to do for the season.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.620
We were doing, I think,
28 for this season.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.580
And the first of the last episode,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.670
we started to sense things were going on.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.790
We changed our production style.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.940
I was separated from customers.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.660
We changed camera angles and stuff

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:16.920
to try to make it all work.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.070
And at the end of that
episode we were given a choice

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:24.130
to, by the network to
either proceed or not.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.400
And we chose to proceed for another week.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.840
But then, about three hours later

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.960
we got the call from the network

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.260
that everything was being closed down.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.140
And that was about 93 days ago.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.299
So we shut that down,
we had eight more left

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:40.320
on the schedule, and everybody
scattered and went home.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.550
- So I don't know what
you'll make of this,

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.940
but I have only watched two reality shows

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.040
in my entire life.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.470
I watched "The Apprentice" with Trump,

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.500
and I watch "Bar Rescue" with you.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.600
So I'm not sure what that means

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.090
about the company that you keep,

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:59.690
but there is something about
"Bar Rescue" that it just,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.210
it checks off all the boxes for me.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:05.790
Because it's very human,
it's dealing with struggle,

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.500
it's dealing with finances,

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.770
it's dealing with, often
with family interactions,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.460
personal interactions,
business, the whole thing.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.930
Where did the idea for the
show originally come from?

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.940
I mean, I know you've been
in that world forever,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.980
but really, the genesis of the show?

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.190
- Well, I've been, as you've said,

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.380
I've been in the industry
for a long, long time.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.380
And I had the most popular seminars

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.010
in the industry before I was on TV.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.720
And I gave a speech in
Vegas at Caesar's Palace,

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.820
and at the end of the speech,

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.487
somebody came up to me, Dave, and said,

00:04:33.487 --> 00:04:35.390
"You should be on TV".

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.847
So I wrote up this thing
that was originally called,

00:04:37.847 --> 00:04:39.730
"On the Rocks", and it was a little

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:40.840
like "Mission Impossible".

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.000
It started with me with files,

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.710
and I'd pull out the mixologist,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.345
and then I'd pull out the chef,

00:04:46.345 --> 00:04:48.020
sort of like "Mission Impossible" started,

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.830
and then I'd go in and do these rescues.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.570
And I knew I had an advantage
because I wasn't a chef.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.140
So I didn't migrate to the kitchen.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.530
I went everywhere.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.600
So I focused much more on other areas

00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.920
of the business than a chef did.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.000
I wrote this up.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.210
And I had done business
with Paramount before

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.490
in the restaurant space as a licensee.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.320
So I went back to my friends
who ran Paramount Television

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.290
with my writeup and I
was really excited, Dave.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.123
And they looked at me, they said,

00:05:13.123 --> 00:05:16.440
"Jon, you'll never be on television.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.930
You're too old, you're
not good looking enough,

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.420
it'll never happen."

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.040
So I went and I shot my own sizzle reel

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.380
the next week with some friends,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.580
in Redondo Beach at a friend's bar

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:31.350
and sent that sizzle reel
to four production companies

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.010
who I had no relationship with.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:35.520
Just emailed it to their
development people.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.140
And I got four offers within three weeks.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.160
So I then navigated through those offers,

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.700
and I wasn't in the television business,

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.040
so I didn't understand run clauses,

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.830
and all the likeness right clauses,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:49.240
and all the things that are particular

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.660
to an entertainment contract.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.240
So I got myself an entertainment manager

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.010
and an entertainment lawyer,

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.730
and we put the deal together,

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.420
and in less than a year from
when that person told me

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.890
I'd never be on TV, the show premiered.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.240
We're now 196 episodes in,

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.720
we're four episodes from 200,

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.750
which is a big record in my industry.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:09.820
Show has generated now, I think it's close

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.730
to $800 million dollars worth of revenue,

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.540
and it's one of the most
successful reality shows

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.373
of all time.

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:16.050
And it just shows, don't give up, Dave.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.100
When somebody else says no, maybe the yes

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.270
should be stronger in your own mind.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.620
- Yeah, well, that's such a
consistent message in the show.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.210
But I'm here in LA.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.080
For people that don't
know how this biz works,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.025
the idea that you sent four blank pitches,

00:06:29.025 --> 00:06:32.230
not that were asked for
or came from an agency

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.120
and got four responses back, I mean,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.910
that's gotta be unheard of.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.460
I assure you, I've sent enough pitches

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.650
to these people to know
that that's pretty good.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.700
- Well, I guess sometimes
it's timing, Dave.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:44.990
But I guess I didn't
realize it at the time,

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.010
but 196 episodes into this, I
guess I'm pretty good at it.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:52.970
And my secret has been
to resist production

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:54.380
from the get go.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.030
And I'm different than a
lot of reality TV stars.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.430
And Trump, I think, was this
was in "Celebrity Apprentice".

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.230
He already had his money.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.050
I already had my success.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.490
So I wasn't gonna sell my
soul to be on television.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.870
It was gonna be real or
I wasn't gonna do it.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:12.580
So the behind-the-scenes in
"Bar Rescue" is almost nil.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.270
I get no briefing, I
don't watch any videos.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.900
I don't go to the places in advance,

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:18.140
I've never met the people before.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.250
I get about a 60-second briefing

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.350
right before I get into the SUV,

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:25.660
and everything else, I
learn it when you do.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:27.450
And I think that's why
the show is successful,

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.663
'cause you'll know fake in a second, Dave.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.120
- Yeah, well, one of the things
I've heard you say is that

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.170
fixing bars is easy,
fixing people is hard.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.520
Did you realize going into
it how much of it was just

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.110
gonna be dealing with, like,
the psychology of people?

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:46.000
Because usually that seems
to be what you're fixing.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.630
You go in and fix the bar after,

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:50.010
but you're really fixing the people first.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.080
- And that was sort of
an evolution, honestly.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.350
Because when I started
this I was a consultant.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.290
And I was a very well-known consultant.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.630
So I had very well-known clients.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.150
So my clients are people
like Marriott Corporation,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:05.050
and Holiday Inns, Inc. and
Fridays, companies like that.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:08.660
So I never knew this level of
failure even existed, Dave.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.240
I never saw anything like
this before in my life.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.430
So when I said to the
network, "I want you guys,

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.040
find me real failures.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.180
I don't want this easy,
let's make this real."

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.120
I never comprehended this.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.550
I mean, to think that
somebody's 50 years old,

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:22.900
a half a million dollars in debt,

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:25.660
living in your parent's
basement with their child,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:29.730
losing $30,000 a month,
out of money, no credit,

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.880
and they're still going, it's remarkable.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.960
And most of these people
have two to three weeks left

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.320
and then they're done anyway.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.700
So it really is a last chance for them.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.230
- So I know I could do the whole hour

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.370
on "Bar Rescue" with you,

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:46.340
but how much of the interactions, like,

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.630
I get you're being real and
you just learned from them.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.520
Do you sense, it's always this thing

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.460
where you see people suddenly
doing these crazy things

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:54.293
right before we started.

00:08:54.293 --> 00:08:56.000
I said I was watching one
yesterday doing cardio

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.220
where there's this woman
who is an owner of a bar,

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.943
she's probably in her mid-50s.

00:08:59.943 --> 00:09:02.625
She's got big boobs, she's
got people doing shots

00:09:02.625 --> 00:09:06.110
off her rack, like, some
of it seems like it's,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:08.150
like, too crazy to be real.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.520
And yet, I suspect is probably isn't.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.840
- Well, no, it's completely real.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.800
Everything is completely real.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.530
There are no actors, there's no scripts,

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:18.840
there's no reshoots.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:20.880
It is completely real.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.730
And when I looked at her,
and I hope I can say this

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.707
on this podcast, when
I look at her and said,

00:09:25.707 --> 00:09:27.280
"I can help your sagging business,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:29.630
I don't know if I can
help your sagging boobs",

00:09:30.781 --> 00:09:32.710
I said that to her for a reason.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.670
I wanted to embarrass her.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:36.460
And when I started "Bar Rescue", Dave,

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:39.530
it was all about dirty bars, and remodels.

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:42.140
And several episodes into it I realized

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.690
how flawed these owners were
to be in those situations.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.970
And if I didn't change them,
I couldn't change anything.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.160
And it was about 120 episodes in,

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.390
believe it or not, I
was in Detroit, Michigan

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.210
with a female owner of a bar

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:58.020
I think I named the Pruding Grounds.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.090
And I looked at her and I
said, "Why are you failing?"

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:03.417
And Dave, she looked at me
in the eyes and said to me,

00:10:03.417 --> 00:10:07.170
"I am failing because
of the euro in Greece."

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.390
This was in suburban Detroit, Michigan.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:14.340
And at that moment I realized
that 120 times earlier

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:16.600
I had asked these business
owners why are they failing,

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.300
and never once, Dave, did anyone ever look

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.700
at me and said failing because of me, Jon.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.270
Not once.

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.310
So I realized, wait a minute.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.920
If she goes home and blames
the euro on her failure,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.060
she has no reason to change.

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:32.987
She'll look in the mirror in
the morning and she'll say,

00:10:32.987 --> 00:10:35.780
"That damn euro taking me down!"

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.127
But if she looked in the mirror and said,

00:10:37.127 --> 00:10:40.320
"I'm failing because of
me", she wouldn't like that.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.820
She would change.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.770
That hit me a couple of years
ago, or three years ago.

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:47.240
And I realized that excuses

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:50.170
are the common denominator of failure.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.097
And then I thought to
myself a little farther,

00:10:52.097 --> 00:10:53.870
"What is an excuse?"

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.350
An excuse is the
reconciliation of a mistake.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:57.580
Either you did something
you shouldn't have,

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:58.960
you didn't do something you should have,

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.830
or you screwed up or you
wouldn't need the excuse.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:06.130
So an excuse is your way of
making yourself feel good

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.540
about your own failure.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:12.530
It's the most paralyzing thing
in business is an excuse.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:14.577
So when I realized that, I said to myself,

00:11:14.577 --> 00:11:17.360
"Wow, I think I found a common
denominator of failure."

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.470
If I can get excuses out of people,

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.950
I can take the things away
from them that paralyze them,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.720
that cause them to hesitate and pause.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:26.787
So then I said,

00:11:26.787 --> 00:11:29.030
"What are the biggest
excuses that we deal with?"

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:30.050
Well, fear is a big one.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.630
I'm scared to do it, I'm scared to do it.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.610
But what we're scared
of, millions of people

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:34.820
have already done.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.760
I'm not talking about standing
on the edge of a cliff,

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.570
obviously, Dave, but in business and risk,

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:41.710
I mean, these aren't new things, fear.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.370
Other people have already done this.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.490
Fear is BS in my view.

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.030
And then I look at scarcity.

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:48.620
Well, I don't have the money.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:51.620
Well, tell that to Steven
Jobs in his garage.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:52.453
Scarcity.

00:11:52.453 --> 00:11:54.410
And then I love the excuse, circumstance.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:55.610
Well, it's a recession.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.920
I can't make money was
the big one years ago.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.100
People got rich during the recession.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.510
A lot of people did.

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:03.260
So during our pandemic,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.280
look at how hand sanitizer companies,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.110
and mask companies, look
at how these industries,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.930
somebody's making money
during all of these things.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.930
And then I looked at the other excuses.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.790
And I came up and I realized that if I can

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:19.800
take these excuses out of people's lives,

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.913
I can stop what paralyzes them.

00:12:22.751 --> 00:12:25.830
That all happened in that Detroit episode.

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.137
And from that I wrote the book,

00:12:27.137 --> 00:12:28.180
"Don't BS Yourself,

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.760
Cut the Excuses That
Are Holding You Back",

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:32.707
which made it to "The New
York Times" Best Seller list,

00:12:32.707 --> 00:12:33.850
and I'm very, very proud of that book,

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.290
because when you finish it,

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.890
excuses won't work for you anymore.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.360
Now you're self-accountable.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:40.820
Now, when you look in a
mirror in the morning,

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.310
you will blame yourself.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.203
And you should.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.720
- Yeah, well, and that's what seems

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.440
to be the consistent message
throughout the shows,

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:51.900
that the owner usually is
making all sorts of excuses

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:52.850
for all sorts of things.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:54.720
And if you can fix that guy or that girl,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.370
then the rest of the
pieces do fall into place.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:59.860
Do you guys ever do look backs

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.350
where you're trying to figure out,

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.360
who actually succeeded after this?

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.150
Or who, the next day after
they go their brand new,

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.500
beautiful bar, just
screwed up the whole thing,

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.720
because I'm sure that happens, too.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.960
- Yeah, there's a website
called "Bar Rescue" Updates.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.170
It's an independent website.

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:15.240
We have nothing to do with it.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.800
It tracks the success of "Bar Rescue".

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:19.570
And of course, now everybody's closed,

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.110
but pre-pandemic, we were tracking

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:25.500
at about 68 to 70% success factor.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.550
Now, I don't know what their
definition of success was,

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.441
Dave, if it's open three
years later, or two years,

00:13:29.441 --> 00:13:31.770
I don't know, but I know
that they had us tracking

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:32.670
at about 68%.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.840
There was also a newspaper article

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.760
that rated the two other
restaurant conversion shows,

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.050
and I won't mention
the shows or the hosts,

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:43.460
but there's three of us that
tend to claim this space.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.170
My show ran about a 68% success factor,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:48.526
the other two were in the 20s.

00:13:48.526 --> 00:13:51.020
So I'm very pleased with what we do.

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.530
I go at it as a consultant
and the cameras are

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:54.880
along for the trip.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.040
I don't go at it as a TV host

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.140
and a consulting is along for the trip.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.630
And I think that's why we're successful.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:02.463
- All right, cool.

00:14:02.463 --> 00:14:04.060
So as I said, I love the show,

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.430
so I could talk about it for an hour.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:06.487
But I do wanna talk
about some other stuff-

00:14:06.487 --> 00:14:07.440
- Oh, to answer your question,

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.273
I didn't answer your question.

00:14:08.273 --> 00:14:11.520
Yes, we did 20 "Back to
the Bar" episodes also.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:12.920
We went back and looked at them.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.590
And I think those are on
the Paramount website.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:16.420
And those were sort of fun, too.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:17.990
- Oh, okay, I'm gonna
have to check those out.

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.110
So you mentioned a little
bit about your career

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:22.280
before all this, and
consulting and all that.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:23.860
When I was doing a little
research this morning,

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.670
I saw that you used to
work at the The Troubadour

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:26.503
in West Hollywood.

00:14:26.503 --> 00:14:27.860
I used to live about a block from there,

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:30.430
I've been to a bunch of
different events over there.

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:32.010
Can you tell me a little
bit about how you got

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.600
into food service, the early
beginnings of your career?

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:38.080
Where your sort of ethos
around personal responsibility

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.856
and the rest of it came from?

00:14:39.856 --> 00:14:43.100
- Well, when I was young, I
was a very serious musician.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.590
I took drum lessons for nine
years and was very good.

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:48.620
I still have a drum set
upstairs in my office.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:49.603
I was very good.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:53.940
I wanted to be a musician.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.480
But my family, and
there's another side of me

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:57.880
that wanted to be going into business.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:58.930
So to make a long story short,

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:00.160
I went to the University of Denver

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:01.630
and majored in political science

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.820
and minored in cultural anthropology,

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:07.120
which I love the study of
primates and what makes us think

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.690
and understanding our
primal behaviors and such.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.500
And I started tending bar in college.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:14.540
And one thing led to another,

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:16.860
and to chase my music career,

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.730
and now that I had a bar tending skill,

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.110
I went to Los Angeles, got an apartment

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:25.210
in the Oakwood Apartments
in Studio City back then,

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:29.894
which had quite a social hot
tub scene back in those days.

00:15:29.894 --> 00:15:32.720
(Dave laughing)
And got a job

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:34.840
in the Troubadour, because
it connected bartending

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:35.853
and music for me.

00:15:36.695 --> 00:15:38.620
And then, evolved to be
manager of the Troubadour,

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.690
and also worked in Barney's
Beanery down the street,

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:41.677
and managed Barney's Beanery there for-

00:15:41.677 --> 00:15:44.220
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, it's still there.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:45.760
- Sure, sure.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.530
So that was my real beginnings.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:50.740
When I took over the Troubadour,

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.180
we had about eight inches of water

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:54.200
on the floor in the kitchen.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.800
It was flooded, and we had no money.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.620
And Doug Weston, the owner,
may he rest in peace,

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.350
back then, had recreational drug habits.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:03.770
And I would close the Troubadour,

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.520
and I'd put $800 or
whatever in the floor safe,

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:08.580
and I'd have to come back
at 8:30 in the morning

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:10.920
to get that $800 out
to pay for the liquor,

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:13.590
'cause everything was COD
'cause we had no credit.

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:15.640
And I'd come in the morning
and the money would be gone,

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.270
'cause Doug took it for
drugs the night before.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:20.220
So I had to run this business

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.660
in the most unfavorable of ways.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.660
I had no money, he kept taking the money.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.250
I started taking it home at night

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:27.880
so he couldn't get it in the morning.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:29.220
I had a flood in the kitchen

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:30.850
that we didn't have the money to fix.

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.620
I got pallets, wooden pallets.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:35.140
We threw 'em on the kitchen floor

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:37.023
so we could walk over the water.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.350
And that's how we opened the Troubadour.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:43.240
And I coordinated something
called the 25th anniversary

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:44.620
of the Troubadour, and we got everybody

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.260
from George Carlin, to Linda Ronstadt,

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.760
all of them to come
back during that month,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:52.010
and we made the money and
stabilized operations.

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.650
- Wow, that's incredible.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.580
So then, from there, where did you go?

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:57.490
What are those missing years between that

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:00.080
and what we know from you now?

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.180
- So from there, actually,
I worked in Barney's Beanery

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:04.410
a short time before the Troubadour.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:05.860
Then went to the Troubadour.

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:08.360
Then from the Troubadour, I got an offer

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:11.300
to be a state manager
for a restaurant chain

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:13.140
out of Florida, called
Beefsteak Charlie's,

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:14.400
believe it or not.
- Oh yeah,

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:15.960
I remember Beefsteak Charlies.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.687
- And they had the huge
salad bar and all that.

00:17:17.687 --> 00:17:21.070
And I thought that was exciting
to learn the food business.

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:21.903
So I went and I did that.

00:17:21.903 --> 00:17:23.010
And I worked down in Florida,

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.180
ran their Fort Lauderdale restaurant

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.450
and a couple of other
restaurants in Florida.

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:29.610
And then I received a phone
call from a head hunter.

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:30.710
And this one changed my life.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:33.540
And I was recruited to go up and be a food

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.070
and beverage director at Grossinger's,

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.380
the largest resort in
America, upstate New York.

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:41.150
1,700 guest rooms, three
nine-hole golf courses,

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.230
our own lake, our own ski mountain,

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.480
America's first snow making equipment.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:46.313
So I went up there and now I'm running-

00:17:46.313 --> 00:17:47.510
- Is that Catskills?

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:49.180
Is that Catskills?
- Catskill Mountains, you bet.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.250
Grossinger's, two biggies were
the Concord and Grossinger's

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.000
up there, so I worked at Grossinger's,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.640
I worked for the family, and
was food and beverage director

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.660
and worked there for a few years.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.190
Really learned the resort business,

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.420
the convention business
and the hotel business.

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:05.800
So now I had run a nightclub, a great bar,

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:08.890
a chain of steakhouses,
and now I'm running food

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.110
and beverage at a large
convention resort hotel.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.300
That rounded out my
experience pretty well.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.210
I got a phone call from
the same head hunger

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.560
a couple years later,
said, "Jon, I got a client

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.660
who's building the greatest
nightclub in the world.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:21.200
He wants you to come run it."

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.550
And his name was Leon
Altemose in Philadelphia,

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.090
and he was creating Pulsations nightclub.

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:29.670
And Pulsations nightclub
had a 4 1/2 ton spaceship,

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.240
27 feet in diameter.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.250
People can Google it online,

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:36.670
that came down and
dropped a $400,000 robot

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:38.000
on the dance floor.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:40.780
And it was the greatest
nightclub in the world.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:42.770
I worked on that for a few years,

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.400
opening and operating
Pulsations nightclub.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:48.280
And now I had run a mega venue.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:53.280
And from Grossinger's, then
I started my own company.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.490
Actually, I'm forgetting a step.

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.860
From Grossinger's, I was hired
by a hotel management company

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:01.280
out of Chicago called P&amp;S Management.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:02.920
And I was a hotel general manager

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.900
and then a Vice President
of Sales and Marketing

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:09.470
for this company that had 28
hotels for about four years.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:12.930
When I left that company, that was 1986,

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:14.930
I started my own consulting company,

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:16.900
'cause tax codes had changed, Dave,

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.090
and hotels couldn't write
off the things they used

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:20.130
to write off now.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:21.550
So restaurants had to make money,

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:23.170
they couldn't be guest amenities anymore.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:24.630
Bars had to make money.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:27.610
These had to be profit
centers, not just amenities.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:30.330
So I left that company,
started my own company,

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:33.870
Taffer Dynamics, and my first
client was that company.

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.070
So they let me start
my company and gave me

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:37.480
a number of contracts.

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:41.860
And then in 1989, I built
my first owned operation

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.850
in St. Louis, a place called Lynn Dickeys

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:45.430
in St. Louis Union Station.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:47.160
And then from there I was approached

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.600
by Simon Property Group
to do a few restaurants

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:51.870
in Mall of America in Minnesota.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.730
So I went up to Minneapolis,
I opened the Alamo Grill,

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:57.370
Gators, an original sports bar,

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.170
another concept that we worked on

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:00.980
up in Mall of America.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:02.770
That one really changed my life.

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:04.240
And then I took Alamo Grill,

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.080
turned it into a chain of steakhouses,

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.610
took it public, then reversed it

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:10.540
into another public company and left

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:12.950
with stock in a huger Canadian entity.

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.200
And then I was in business.

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:15.770
I had owned, I had taken in public,

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:19.090
I had run hotels, bars,
restaurants, concert venues.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.063
I had taken companies public,
taken companies profit.

00:20:23.230 --> 00:20:24.110
I was really ready.

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:25.860
And that, of course, was a long time ago.

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:27.810
So since then, I've consulted

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:29.510
to some of the greatest
companies in the world.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:32.390
So I always tell people
during my consulting practice

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:35.650
in young years, I made every
mistake there was to make.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.470
And that's why I got really good at it.

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:38.420
And now I am.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:40.740
I'm probably one of the
best at it in the world.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.110
It's funny, 'cause when I hear you talk

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.960
about your resume, it's like, wow,

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.220
the fact that I really only know this guy

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.700
as a sort of reality TV host,

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:51.140
but it's a lot of decades of stuff.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.240
I mean, you just went across
the food service industry

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:56.110
and did pretty much...

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:57.380
Is there anything you didn't do?

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:00.020
Like, do you see any
holes in that industry

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:03.230
that you really didn't
do or weren't part of?

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:04.920
- No, I know every type of venue

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:06.860
from concert venues to outdoor arenas,

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:09.430
inside venues, fine dining restaurants,

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.440
casual dining, fast food.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:13.820
I mean, I've been in
every one of these sectors

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:16.710
as either an owner,
operator, management company

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:19.730
or consultant, I really
do know every aspect

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:20.563
of this business.

00:21:20.563 --> 00:21:22.680
And make no mistake, Dave, that helps.

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:24.370
And I think it's very
important that people

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:26.860
in casual dining have an
understanding of upscale dining

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:28.540
because they impact each other.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.290
And there are things in service sequencing

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:32.930
and things that one can learn
in one segment from another,

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:34.480
or one category from another.

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.570
Fast food is another great example.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:38.850
The transactional style
of fast food now has

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:40.664
to exist in full service restaurants

00:21:40.664 --> 00:21:42.681
because of COVID and contactless.

00:21:42.681 --> 00:21:44.190
And so I think that broadness

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:45.610
of appeal has really helped me,

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.940
particularly my hotel
background and resort background

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:49.850
has been really helpful for me.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:54.070
- Can you talk about just
some of the basic principles

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:56.770
that you try to bring
to any of the businesses

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:58.730
that you're coaching or working on?

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:00.220
Because as I said, since we're doing this

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:01.650
as small business week, we're gonna talk

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:04.110
to people, not just in
the food service industry,

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.720
but just some of the
principles that you think

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:08.600
actually build solid, strong businesses

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:10.490
that can actually survive when we go

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:12.130
through terrible situations

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:14.215
like we're going through right now.

00:22:14.215 --> 00:22:16.250
- I think that a couple of things.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:18.800
One, I don't think that
you're doing a podcast

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:21.153
or a broadcast right now,
I don't believe that.

00:22:21.153 --> 00:22:22.390
I don't believe that's your product.

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:23.690
I believe that's your vehicle.

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:25.690
Your product is reactions.

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:28.140
If your audience doesn't
react to what you do,

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:29.800
then it serves no purpose.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.470
So you're not in the content business,

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:32.910
you're in the reaction business.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:35.600
Content is your vehicle,
it's not your product.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.530
That's the first thing I
learned when I was younger.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:39.790
That cook in the kitchen
is not making an entree.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:41.220
He's not making a product.

00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:42.680
He's creating a reaction.

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.040
He's the vehicle.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:45.700
When that plate hits the table, Dave,

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:46.750
one of two things happens.

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:50.960
Either you sit up and you
react to it, or you don't.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.480
That reaction is the product.

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:55.540
So I suggest the cell phones companies

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:57.520
don't sell technology,
they sell reactions.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.270
They achieve it through technology.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.080
I suggest that every business
is in the reaction business.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.010
I own the term reaction management.

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:05.640
My first book, "Raise the Bar"

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:08.380
was all about reaction
management and understanding.

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:09.213
And this comes out

00:23:09.213 --> 00:23:11.570
of my cultural anthropology
training as well.

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:14.330
Understanding the primal
instincts of what you react to

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:15.560
as a consumer.

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:17.680
So I believe that we don't play music,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.650
we play reactions, we
achieve it through music.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:21.330
We don't make drinks, we make reactions.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:22.530
We achieve it through drinks.

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:25.210
So I am in the business of
creating human reactions,

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:26.640
period, end of discussion.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.890
And he or she who creates
the best reactions

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:31.560
in my business wins, end of story.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.150
That's the business that we're in.

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:36.070
Now, we can dissect
that into connectivity,

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:39.040
and engagement, and pace, and environment.

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:41.780
But every one of those things
has to create reaction.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:43.690
Pace is about reaction.

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.210
Go to a fine steakhouse.

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:47.070
They walk really slow.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.010
Go to a Denny's, they walk really fast.

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.490
If the waiter walks too
fast in a fine steakhouse

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:54.090
that steak isn't worth $80 anymore, is it?

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.450
So these things all affect reactions.

00:23:57.450 --> 00:24:00.150
And reactions drive consumer behavior.

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:02.360
Reactions drive transactions.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:03.193
Period.

00:24:03.193 --> 00:24:04.670
It isn't the other way around.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:08.250
So when I take a look at the
whole science of reactions,

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:10.820
then in our corporate training,
we created something else

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:13.290
as a term that we own called GROWs.

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:16.840
GROW stands for guest
reaction opportunity windows.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:18.870
In other businesses we call it CROWs.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:21.100
Customer reaction opportunity windows.

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:23.690
Points where I can
create a reaction in you.

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:26.670
I spec 'em, I design 'em
right into the business.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:30.510
I wanna design this
right into that business.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:33.630
So I'm designing, not a plate,
I'm designing a reaction.

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:35.720
And I'll design that
plate 100 frickin' times

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:37.350
until you sit up.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.470
And that's the business
that we are all in.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:43.170
And he or she, no matter
what business that we're in,

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.400
are gonna create the
best reactions of all.

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:47.330
Now, if we take a look
at the current situation

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:48.420
we're right now, if we can talk

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:50.220
about the pandemic for
a moment or two, Dave.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:52.990
- Yeah, absolutely.
- I said this four months ago,

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:54.450
and I was one of the first ones to say it,

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:57.220
and research now shows that I was 2% off.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:24:58.810
When this started I said, people are gonna

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:00.290
come back in thirds.

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:01.910
The first third is the quick 1/3.

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:04.380
They're gonna come right out
again when the doors open.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:07.260
They're younger, they're
a little fearless.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.940
They're gonna come out,
they might not wear a mask.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:11.020
We're seeing that first 1/3 now.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:14.070
According to research, it's 31%.

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:15.610
I projected 1/3.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:18.330
The second 1/3 I call the reserved 1/3.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:19.810
They wanna watch for a couple weeks.

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:21.220
They wanna see what happens.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:22.260
Is there a surge?

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:23.520
How do the restaurants look?

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:24.353
Does it look safe?

00:25:24.353 --> 00:25:26.090
Are people wearing masks, are they not?

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:27.840
If it feels good and looks good,

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:29.270
the reserved 1/3 will go out.

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:30.780
Now we've got 2/3.

00:25:30.780 --> 00:25:33.696
The third 1/3 is the certain 1/3.

00:25:33.696 --> 00:25:36.870
They're not going anywhere
until they are certain

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:38.090
that they're safe.

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:42.000
So a vaccine, a rock solid treatment needs

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.960
to emerge before they'll come out.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:47.043
The issue is that the
third 1/3 has the money.

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:49.990
It tends to be an older demographic,

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:51.360
a more affluent demographic,

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:53.200
greater disposable income number.

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:55.610
They tend to feed the luxury sector

00:25:55.610 --> 00:25:58.130
more than the first
1/3 and the second 1/3.

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:00.090
So there's a demographic reality

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:02.050
to this as these businesses
shuffle through it.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:04.620
For example, I'm here in Las Vegas,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:05.740
we've been open for about a week.

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:07.100
We're doing pretty well.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:09.360
But we're finding the
first customers who come

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:11.440
are value-oriented.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:13.710
They're coming, they're that first 1/3,

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:15.810
they're younger, they
don't have high dispose,

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.410
so they're very value-oriented,

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.800
so we better come out of the
gate with value propositions.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:22.940
When the second 1/3 comes out-

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:23.940
- What are the kinds of things?

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:25.990
What are the kinds of things
that you guys are doing

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:29.670
in Vegas, or that you're seeing
being done to address that?

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:30.860
- Well, the average casino has

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:34.170
about 800 policy changes in it.

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:36.870
From non-invasive temperature
testing automatically

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:38.720
as you walk through the
door of the property,

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.980
to contactless check-in,
to only one or two people

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:44.300
in elevators and are policed and managed

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:46.720
at each elevator floor,
to sanitized chips,

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:49.250
and cards, and when you're
finished with a slot machine

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:52.130
it turns off and it can't turn on again

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:53.280
until it's sanitized.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:55.300
And there's a lock and a key system on it.

00:26:55.300 --> 00:26:59.610
So they've done all sorts of
things to make it successful.

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:02.220
But you know, Dave, what worries me is,

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:03.790
when we look at these thirds,

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:06.100
obviously there's a progress
we need to go through.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:07.890
And let me ask you a question if I can.

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:09.470
If you were gonna go out to dinner tonight

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.327
in a restaurant that was open,

00:27:11.327 --> 00:27:12.850
and there was a crowd
of people in that front

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:15.410
of that restaurant, and none
of them were wearing masks,

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:16.243
would you go in?

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:18.510
- So it's interesting.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:20.060
So I'll tell ya, I have only been

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.200
to one restaurant since this all happened.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:25.010
It was last week, it was a
restaurant right by my house,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:27.930
just a burger joint bar that
I go to every now and again.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:30.400
The waitress, I was not
wearing a mask there,

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:31.850
and it was pretty sporadic.

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:33.760
So I was with one person.

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:37.350
The waitress was wearing
a mask and a face guard.

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:40.100
And it's not that comfortable.

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:40.933
You know this.

00:27:40.933 --> 00:27:43.680
You need to read people's
faces to feel comfortable,

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.700
it feels sort of weird with all that.

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:49.820
I think for me at this
point as a relatively young,

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:52.880
healthy person, if I went
to a nice restaurant,

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:55.000
let's say in Beverly Hills
or something like that,

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.490
I would be okay if people
were not wearing stuff.

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:00.330
I actually would be okay with that.

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:02.860
But I don't consider
myself in the last 1/3

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:03.780
that you just talked about.

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:06.600
I think I'm somewhere
between the first 1/3

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:08.580
and the second 1/3, I think.

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.840
And also, here is Los Angeles
where we've been closed

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:13.830
for so long, I think
I'm also having, like,

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.050
a reverse reaction,

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:16.250
which I think a lot of people are having,

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:18.200
which is like, let's just go.

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:20.746
Let's just go and see what happens, yeah.

00:28:20.746 --> 00:28:24.530
- I worry about irresponsibility.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:26.200
Not on the part of restaurant owners.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:27.760
I'm concerned, Dave,

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.320
that a restaurant does everything right,

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.713
it sanitizes, puts their
employees in the proper gear,

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:35.340
does all the procedures in the kitchen,

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:39.500
all the PPE attire, has
everything exactly right,

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.370
but then a bunch of customers
come in without masks,

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:46.400
create a bad impression,
pictures go on social media

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:49.170
of this place packed, and
then all of the portion

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:52.910
of society that wants to wear
masks will never go there.

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:55.420
So there's a real challenge
for business today.

00:28:55.420 --> 00:28:56.730
And I think I know the answer,

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:59.610
but I'm not quite sure
if my answer is right.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:00.640
And this is deep, Dave,

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:03.040
if you were in a bar
business and you said, okay,

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:05.110
I'm gonna play country music.

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:07.230
No rock-and-roll people are coming.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:09.210
You've made a decision
that's gonna completely

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:12.350
segment your business
to country people only.

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:15.060
So you're gonna see some boots,
you're gonna see some hats,

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:17.360
you're not gonna see a lot
of Armani suits, probably,

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.440
and it's gonna be a country environment.

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:21.410
Let's say you chose, ah,
I don't wanna do that.

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:23.320
I'm gonna play rock-and-roll in my bar.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:25.740
Okay, no country people there.

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:27.030
No hip hop people there.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:28.910
You just segmented your business

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.780
to a portion of the population.

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:32.390
Let's say you say, I'm gonna play hip hop.

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:34.570
Same deal, no country, no rock,

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:36.940
you segmented yourself
into a certain segment

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:38.250
of the population.

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:39.630
My point is this.

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:43.560
There's the mask wearers and
there's the non-mask wearers.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:46.260
And I think that
restaurants are gonna be put

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:49.150
in a position where they're
gonna have to choose.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:51.310
Just like that music segmentation.

00:29:51.310 --> 00:29:53.120
Either you have to wear
a mask in my restaurant

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:54.990
when you're not eating,
you have to wear a mask

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:58.100
when you're wearing outside, or you don't.

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:01.650
But I don't think mixing
it is gonna work long-term.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.320
And that's a really tough choice.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.950
And nobody's talking about
this is the industry.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:07.620
Do it or don't do it.

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:08.970
What do we have to do?

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:10.570
And it's a segmenting decision,

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:13.110
just like music is, or price points are.

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:16.730
So I'm of the belief that
restaurants need to pick.

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.270
And I'm of the belief for
long-term sustainability.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:20.360
And here's what I worry about.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:23.390
We are living in a culture
of accountability today.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:25.520
People go out, have a bad
meal, it's all over Yelp

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:26.900
in a matter of minutes.

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:27.940
Right?

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:30.040
So if people get sick in a restaurant

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:32.060
and it's traced back
to a restaurant, done.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.810
Case closed, that place
will never open again.

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:37.200
In New York, they're gonna
take liquor licenses away

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:39.990
if customers act irresponsibly.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:42.850
So we're at a turning
point as an industry.

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:45.210
Are we gonna allow the
behavior of customers

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.430
to destroy our investment?

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:49.740
Or are we gonna regulate that behavior?

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:51.870
The nightclub business has
been doing this for years

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:55.050
by segmenting by music,
segmenting by dress codes,

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:56.300
and doing segmentation.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.060
We've survived in a segmented world

00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:00.640
since I got in this business
all these years ago.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:03.220
I don't believe that we
can succeed as an industry

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.620
if we don't choose what side we're on.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:09.100
And if we choose to run in
an unprotected environment,

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.710
and choose to not do
some of these processes

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:13.420
that can keep people safe,

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:15.550
we may pay the ultimate price.

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:17.770
And the ultimate price is not
only getting somebody sick

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:19.300
or potentially killing them,

00:31:19.300 --> 00:31:21.270
but destroying your business.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:23.210
So I take an opposite position to you.

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:26.280
And I'm not sure that I would let you

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:29.860
in my restaurant or stand in
front of it without a mask.

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.071
- Yeah, and I totally hear you.

00:31:32.071 --> 00:31:34.740
It's a personal choice, not only

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:37.490
for the individual consumer, but obviously

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.910
for the owner of the bar or restaurant.

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:41.330
Do you think what makes
it even more muddled,

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:44.180
what confuses it even more
is that it's one thing

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:46.230
if you're walking into a clothing store

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:47.090
and you're wearing a mask,

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:48.600
well, you're not using your mouth.

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:50.410
You're not using your face.

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:52.000
At a restaurant it's
like, well, wait a minute.

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.240
I can walk in, I have to
wear a mask, let's say,

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.670
when I walk in, but we
do know I have to drink

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:58.740
through my mouth, I have
to eat through my mouth.

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.270
So there is these sort of inconsistencies

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:02.360
where it's like, people are just like,

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:03.700
well, then, why am I doing it at all?

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:07.160
Like, it's just the natural
questions that arise.

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:09.360
- Well, if there's spacing at the tables

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.990
and people are standing
in front of the restaurant

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:12.420
without that level of spacing,

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:13.730
I think it's reasonable for us to say,

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.323
we'd appreciate you wearing
a mask until you're seated.

00:32:17.416 --> 00:32:19.760
Seems like a simple definition.

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:21.360
My worry is this.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:23.470
If I had, and I said this on "Bar Rescue",

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:25.810
if there's 60 motorcycles
in front of a bar,

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:28.600
there's a bunch of people
that won't go to that bar.

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.470
If there's a bunch of people dressed

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:32.130
in gang colors in front of a bar,

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:33.740
there's people who won't go in that bar.

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:35.520
Today, if there's a bunch of people

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:37.730
in front of a bar that look irresponsible

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:39.780
as it relates to COVID,
there are customers

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:40.910
that won't go in that bar.

00:32:40.910 --> 00:32:42.450
It's no different.

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:45.290
So when you say about how
are we gonna be successful,

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:46.140
there's two issues.

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:47.630
There's that issue.

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:50.520
And all of that is driven
by this one premise.

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:52.120
And bear with me for a minute, Dave.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:53.810
If you, you're a burger guy, right?

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:55.800
You love a good burger?
- Yeah, oh yeah, oh yeah.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:59.040
- So let's say that your
favorite burger place in town

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:02.200
after this pandemic, you go walk up to it,

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:03.330
everybody's packed in there,

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:04.770
they're not wearing masks,

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.110
they're not even wearing
masks behind the counter,

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.270
the whole thing just doesn't feel right.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:10.760
But two blocks farther away

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:14.070
is your second favorite
burger place, father,

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:15.510
but they're clean, they're tight,

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:18.340
their staff is in masks, I
mean this place is together.

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:20.220
It looks great, it's safe.

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:22.510
You're now gonna go to your
second favorite burger place

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:25.120
which is farther away than
you're first burger place.

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:28.260
Dave, that changes everything.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:31.670
Trust has now ratcheted to the top.

00:33:31.670 --> 00:33:33.450
So now you're gonna walk farther

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:35.820
to go to your second
favorite burger place,

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:37.930
'cause your first favorite burger place

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:39.653
didn't gain your trust.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:41.513
That's powerful.
- Yeah, well,

00:33:41.513 --> 00:33:44.380
it's interesting because
you're talking about

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:47.510
free market capitalism, and
also talking about rules.

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:51.000
But ultimately you're
saying it's on people

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:52.770
to make the right choices.

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:56.070
- So, if in fact, trust is ratcheting up

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:59.920
to the top of the motivation
list of what businesses

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.670
I choose to do business with,

00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:03.000
how do we build trust?

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:04.160
And this goes back to your question,

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:05.840
how do we get successful now?

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:08.050
Well, if we know that we have thirds,

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:10.350
then if we know we have to build trust,

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:12.160
even for you who's quicker to go out,

00:34:12.160 --> 00:34:16.240
maybe, than I am, then
how do we build trust?

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:19.780
Well, trust isn't words, trust
isn't advertising campaigns.

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:22.030
Trust is transparency.

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:23.730
You begin to trust me as an individual

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.020
once you get to know me a little, right?

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:27.010
Trust is built.

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:29.360
So trust is a very difficult emotion

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:30.880
to build in a person.

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:33.450
So we need to be transparent.

00:34:33.450 --> 00:34:36.480
So the way we create transparency is

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:39.110
by creating visual
standards of what people see

00:34:39.110 --> 00:34:40.740
and what they can expect in our business.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:43.170
So for example, a few weeks
ago on my social media,

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:45.440
Dave, and some guy's making
lasagna in a kitchen.

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:47.130
And he posts a picture of a cook,

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:49.060
and he's standing in this
stainless steel line,

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:51.850
and he's laying a lasagna
noodle in the pan.

00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:54.770
And the post says, "Our
famous lasagna will be ready

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:57.560
for curbside pickup at five o'clock."

00:34:57.560 --> 00:34:59.590
Pretty noble effort, right?

00:34:59.590 --> 00:35:01.500
Guy's not wearing a mask.

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:03.960
He's wearing a ball cap from home.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:07.140
He's wearing clear, cheap plastic gloves

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:09.750
that somebody wears in a
salon when they do hair color.

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:11.650
It's not even food service rated.

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:14.210
All the imaging is wrong.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:17.060
So if we don't build trust,

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:19.750
nobody's gonna eat that frickin' lasagna.

00:35:19.750 --> 00:35:22.650
So we as an industry
have to really understand

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:24.230
how to build trust.

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:27.160
What are the marketing
communications of trust?

00:35:27.160 --> 00:35:29.470
When you walk up to the
front door of my business,

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:33.160
retail, restaurant, bar,
what looks different?

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:35.160
Oh, they changed this.

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:36.870
And subliminally you're saying,

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:38.220
well, they changed it for me.

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:39.360
They're keeping me safe.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:40.280
This is a good thing.

00:35:40.280 --> 00:35:41.530
Oh, I feel good about this.

00:35:41.530 --> 00:35:42.363
I like this.

00:35:42.363 --> 00:35:45.400
Okay, from one little thing,

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.860
all of these assumptions
go through your head.

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:49.810
So what has to be different?

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:52.720
I suggest that every business
needs to change something.

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:54.220
They need to change their seating layout,

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:55.840
they need to change signage in the front,

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.400
they need to put a podium
out front for check in,

00:35:58.400 --> 00:36:00.780
they need to do something
that sends the message

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:03.130
of trust in a transparent way.

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:06.670
If I can send the message
of trust in this world,

00:36:06.670 --> 00:36:08.716
I can get the first 1/3, the second 1/3,

00:36:08.716 --> 00:36:09.870
and the third 1/3.

00:36:09.870 --> 00:36:13.910
If trust, if that first
1/3 slams out social media

00:36:13.910 --> 00:36:16.420
with untrusting visuals, you're gonna have

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:19.720
a hell of a time getting
into second and third 1/3.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:23.070
- So where do you think
government and regulation

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:24.520
kind of fit into this?

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:27.650
Because we're hearing all
sorts of arbitrary things,

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:29.360
or seemingly arbitrary things every week,

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:30.640
depending on where you live.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:34.340
Okay, we can be at 50%
restaurant capacity,

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:36.190
but that's regardless whether you've

00:36:36.190 --> 00:36:38.180
done all of these precautions or not

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:39.270
done all of these precautions.

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:40.570
And I'm sure you must be hearing

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:42.480
from owners of restaurants and bars

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.710
who are saying hey, I'll do everything

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:45.940
that I possibly can do.

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:47.090
I'll put all the investment in,

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:48.210
we'll do all the work,

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:51.230
but if we can only open at 50% capacity,

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:53.810
there's no way we can succeed regardless

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:56.520
of doing everything or
not doing everything.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.130
- Well, and I don't disagree with you.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:00.060
So new revenue centers need to be found,

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:01.850
hence delivery, curbside pickup.

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.830
But these third party delivery apps

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:05.553
are destroying our industry.

00:37:05.553 --> 00:37:06.800
And they're the only
people who make money.

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:10.130
Pay them 25, up to 35%
third party delivery apps.

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:12.740
So I beg people on this podcast,

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:13.810
if you wanna do a to-go order

00:37:13.810 --> 00:37:15.070
or a curbside delivery order,

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:16.130
call the restaurant directly.

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:18.230
Don't call one of these
third party sources.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:20.020
It's a huge deal to them.

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:22.890
It's a huge difference in
the way they make money.

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:25.320
So we have to create
these new opportunities

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:26.340
for revenue models.

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:27.620
So I have a friend, Tiffany Derry,

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:30.890
you've seen her, chef Tiffany
Derry on "Bar Rescue".

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:33.180
Her restaurant now does family meal packs

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:35.090
for four to go.

00:37:35.090 --> 00:37:37.210
And for $39 you get an amazing meal

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:39.950
with potatoes, and vegetables,
and chicken, and soup.

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:43.610
So she's come up with a new
way to package her product.

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:46.530
We take a look at, I
was on a consulting call

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:48.360
with a big brewery in Canada,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:50.720
and they have a
merchandising program called

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:52.420
From Tap to Table.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:53.440
Doesn't work anymore.

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:55.113
Now, it's From Tap to Home.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:57.560
We gotta get our brand home.

00:37:57.560 --> 00:37:59.000
We gotta get the delivery packaging.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:00.690
We gotta get our brand to move.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:03.600
So I'm with you, Dave,
but here's the problem.

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:05.420
I'm at 50% capacity,

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:08.040
so I have to, if I'm at half the capacity,

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:09.993
I have to double my hours of revenue.

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:12.440
So an hour and a half for lunch

00:38:12.440 --> 00:38:13.390
doesn't work for me anymore.

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:15.020
I need three hours of lunch now.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:17.240
So I need to do early bird lunch specials,

00:38:17.240 --> 00:38:19.720
late bird lunch specials,
early bird dinner.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:24.210
I gotta generate more hours
to offset my loss in capacity.

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:26.270
So I gotta look at
that, which means I have

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:28.020
to do some promotional activity.

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:30.290
Free this, free that, get this $2 off

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:32.220
to drive more hours of traffic.

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:35.535
The problem is, I've also
increased my labor costs,

00:38:35.535 --> 00:38:36.880
'cause now I'm doing in three hours

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:38.790
what I used to do in one
hour, and hour and a half.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:41.480
So it affects the whole economic model.

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:42.690
Here's my point.

00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:45.500
We have two challenges in
front of us as an industry.

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:48.660
One is to sustain ourselves during this.

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:50.840
And the second is to have the resources

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.270
to launch properly when it ends.

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:55.800
If we spend all of our
money sustaining ourselves,

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:59.540
Dave, we are never gonna reopen
with the resources to win.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:02.390
If we hold back our sustaining dollars now

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:04.770
and hold them for when
we can open properly,

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:07.400
I personally think that's
the better scenario.

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.230
And I've found for very
of my many friends,

00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:10.930
sure, we wanna keep our employees working,

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:12.430
we wanna do everything we can.

00:39:12.430 --> 00:39:15.610
But it might make sense
to close for a few weeks

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:17.760
and open with a real marketing plan,

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:20.070
and an activation plan,
and a promotion plan,

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.820
and open with a bang later when we have

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:24.130
the potential to do so.

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:25.910
It's hard to spend the
money to do anything

00:39:25.910 --> 00:39:28.930
with a bang when you're
locked at 50% capacity.

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:32.970
The other thing that you said
which was important to me was,

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:34.640
what do we do, what are the standards?

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:37.750
25%, 50%, blah, blah, blah.

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:40.080
So I think it's paramount,

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:42.470
and I know the NRA, National
Restaurant Association

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:45.190
not rifle, I know that the NRA...

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:48.650
I always get hate notes when
I put NRA on social media.

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:50.600
- I had a buddy in the
food service industry

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:52.430
who was always telling me
to go into the NRA meetings.

00:39:52.430 --> 00:39:53.900
I think they were in Chicago,

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:55.770
and it was always very confusing,

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:56.797
but we figured it out.

00:39:56.797 --> 00:39:57.630
- Yes. But you know what?

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:59.630
They've put together
some wonderful programs

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:03.500
and standards that we can follow
to assure consumer safety.

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:06.280
I think that there needs to
be some federal standard.

00:40:06.280 --> 00:40:07.590
I do, I believe that.

00:40:07.590 --> 00:40:09.320
And the problem with the governors today,

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.220
tangle with the federal government now,

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:13.690
there's an ego thing, there's
a push and pull in power,

00:40:13.690 --> 00:40:15.300
we're more divisive than ever before.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:17.100
So a democratic governor
is not gonna listen

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:19.630
to a republican president no
matter what the standards are.

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:22.570
If he says 50%, they're doing 25.

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:24.310
And I'm not being political here.

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:26.870
But there are standards that make sense,

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:29.370
and the disease can be
contracted just as easily

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:31.520
in Florida as it can Oklahoma.

00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:33.030
There's no chemical difference

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:34.710
between the disease and those places.

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:37.380
So if there was a standard that was safe,

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:39.840
a national standard, you and I could start

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:42.623
to feel comfortable with that standard.

00:40:43.494 --> 00:40:46.010
So I think that that's the
problem is you don't know

00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:47.290
what to expect.

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:50.060
And every state is a
free-for-all in standards.

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:52.980
I think we need a national program, I do.

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:54.890
And I'm just not sure the
states would agree to it,

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:57.020
but I'm pushing for it very heavily

00:40:57.020 --> 00:41:00.170
that defines space requirements,
distancing requirements,

00:41:00.170 --> 00:41:02.640
food service, back of
the house requirements,

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:05.713
so that everybody knows that
it's an even playing field.

00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:08.620
Every restaurant is in
the same playing field,

00:41:08.620 --> 00:41:11.760
the same economic modeling,
the same consumer presence,

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:12.610
it all changes.

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:15.750
Years ago, Dave, I can remember
when Tempe, Arizona put

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.940
a no smoking policy in place.

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:19.500
It was a little town.

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:21.410
Nobody else around it did.

00:41:21.410 --> 00:41:23.570
Every bar went broke when that happened.

00:41:23.570 --> 00:41:24.810
You can't do things.

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:27.330
Guy that lives on the
Tennessee/Kentucky border,

00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:29.170
the guy in Tennessee is making a fortune,

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:30.500
the guy in Kentucky is starving.

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:31.533
They're across the street from each other,

00:41:31.533 --> 00:41:34.110
just the regulations are different.

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:36.120
That doesn't work in the
business environment.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.980
I think we need to
level the playing field,

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:40.840
create some national
standard of behavior for us

00:41:40.840 --> 00:41:42.530
that's predictable to you.

00:41:42.530 --> 00:41:44.310
So you know whether you
go to Dave's restaurant

00:41:44.310 --> 00:41:46.370
or Jon's restaurant, it's gonna have

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:48.140
the same policies in place.

00:41:48.140 --> 00:41:50.923
We have to get to that
point somehow, I believe.

00:41:50.923 --> 00:41:53.811
Do you?
- Do you think when we get,

00:41:53.811 --> 00:41:55.385
- I'm curious to hear your opinion.

00:41:55.385 --> 00:41:57.260
- So, well, it's interesting.

00:41:57.260 --> 00:41:59.940
As a general rule, I'm a
big states' right guys.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:01.420
So I want the states to be able

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:03.700
to set whatever regulations as possible,

00:42:03.700 --> 00:42:06.280
because then the experiment
sort of bounces around.

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:08.160
But as I say on this show all the time,

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:11.370
something like coronavirus
or any virus doesn't respond

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:12.720
to states' borders,

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:16.230
so I think we need something
that blends both state

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:20.390
and federal would be my
short answer on that.

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:21.223
- And I'm with you.

00:42:21.223 --> 00:42:22.056
I'm very state rights oriented,

00:42:22.056 --> 00:42:23.910
and local as well.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:25.150
But I think in this position,

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:26.480
the problem is that the actions,

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:27.540
and I'm just picking Tennessee,

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.140
I have no issues with Tennessee,

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:31.220
the issues, if somebody works in Tennessee

00:42:31.220 --> 00:42:33.330
and lives in Kentucky,
the policies in Tennessee

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:35.410
could be impacted in
the state of Kentucky.

00:42:35.410 --> 00:42:38.040
So I think there is a
cross responsibility here,

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:39.690
particularly near the borders

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.210
that we have some standards
of behaviors so that no state,

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:45.490
town, or person is disadvantaged.

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:47.500
That seems reasonable to me.

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:48.960
- Yeah, well, it's always a matter of,

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:50.390
how do you get there?

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:52.440
I suspect we all wanna
get to the same thing,

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:54.840
which is people back to work
and enjoying themselves.

00:42:54.840 --> 00:42:58.010
Do you think, regardless of how the third,

00:42:58.010 --> 00:42:59.700
third, third shake out,

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:02.670
I can sort of see everything
going one of two ways.

00:43:02.670 --> 00:43:04.830
One way would be, which would be terrible

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:06.770
for the economy and certainly

00:43:06.770 --> 00:43:08.860
for the food service industry would be

00:43:08.860 --> 00:43:11.480
that people have now just new behaviors.

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:13.790
We're used to bringing
in food all the time,

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:16.850
most of us are cooking
more one way or another,

00:43:16.850 --> 00:43:19.650
and that that behavior
will just sort of continue.

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:20.757
Or there's another part of me,

00:43:20.757 --> 00:43:23.020
and I hope it goes more to this,

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.700
where when we really are
on the other side of this,

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:28.280
that there's more of a
roaring '20s feeling.

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:29.350
We are in the '20s now,

00:43:29.350 --> 00:43:31.360
and there will be more
of a feeling of, like,

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:33.480
yeah, let's remember what it was like

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:36.780
to go out there and go
to a bar and hang out

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:38.630
with your friends and
meet some new people,

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:40.600
or go to your favorite restaurant.

00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:43.730
I'm really missing BOA
Steakhouse on Sunset.

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:46.310
Like, to do that kinda stuff,

00:43:46.310 --> 00:43:50.080
do you sense we will go
one way or the other?

00:43:50.080 --> 00:43:50.913
- I do.

00:43:50.913 --> 00:43:52.580
But I think it might get
split by those thirds

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:53.900
a little bit.

00:43:53.900 --> 00:43:57.010
I think that the sting
is gonna last longer

00:43:57.010 --> 00:43:58.940
for people of an older demo.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:43:59.840
I just do.

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:02.460
And I think that if I
were to do a prediction,

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:04.160
I think the luxury dining sector is gonna

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:05.980
be the most challenged.

00:44:05.980 --> 00:44:07.720
I think they're gonna be
the last ones to come out.

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:08.553
Even though they tend

00:44:08.553 --> 00:44:10.270
to have more spread dining rooms anyway,

00:44:10.270 --> 00:44:11.630
they probably lose less seating

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:13.770
with six-foot separation
because the tables

00:44:13.770 --> 00:44:16.600
aren't bunched like in a
casual dining environment.

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:18.770
I'm very concerned, there's
a way to look at this, too.

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:22.620
Let's say we do lose 30 to 40%
of our restaurants and bars.

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:26.030
Well, in theory, we now
have 40% less capacity

00:44:26.030 --> 00:44:30.090
and seating, but we have
about the same marketplace.

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:33.150
So I think that the ones that survive

00:44:33.150 --> 00:44:37.490
might actually survive into
a very vibrant marketplace.

00:44:37.490 --> 00:44:40.580
And I think I agree with you
on a roaring '20s analogy.

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:42.710
But I think we need to get to a vaccine

00:44:42.710 --> 00:44:46.410
and a place where that
safety is somewhat assured.

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:49.690
- How worried are you
that small businesses,

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:52.270
the guy that just wants
to make a great bar,

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:54.540
a great restaurant, not a franchise,

00:44:54.540 --> 00:44:57.670
not a big business, but the guy
that wants, in his hometown,

00:44:57.670 --> 00:45:01.190
to just put the greatest
restaurant ever there.

00:45:01.190 --> 00:45:03.010
That they will have
looked at what happened

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:05.230
over the last year and
be like, you know what?

00:45:05.230 --> 00:45:07.810
The risk, risks always, I'm
sure you know the numbers

00:45:07.810 --> 00:45:09.060
for restaurants and bars,

00:45:09.060 --> 00:45:11.830
if you wanna tell everybody
the failure rates,

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:14.050
they're always absurdly high.

00:45:14.050 --> 00:45:17.460
But now, the risks seem
almost incalculably high

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:19.210
for people to wanna get in on this.

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:21.410
- It's interesting that you mention.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:23.920
I own a franchise called Taffer's Tavern.

00:45:23.920 --> 00:45:25.360
And we created this franchise,

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:26.250
you'll find this interesting,

00:45:26.250 --> 00:45:27.780
and I'm not doing this as a plug.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:30.020
We, two years ago, had no labor pool.

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:32.490
We were approaching $15 minimum wages,

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:33.990
and the labor pool that we did have

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:36.900
were mostly new Americans who
didn't speak English so well.

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:39.440
So I said, "I gotta create
the casual dining concept

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:40.273
of the future."

00:45:40.273 --> 00:45:41.700
So I wanna cut labor costs in half,

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:43.650
I wanna bring in robotic cooking,

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:46.840
robotic ovens, eliminate
back of the house,

00:45:46.840 --> 00:45:49.600
create this whole new restaurant model.

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:50.550
So I did.

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:52.610
I worked with companies like Middlby

00:45:52.610 --> 00:45:54.610
and worked in test kitchens
around the country,

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:57.970
and created this kitchen of
the future concept a year ago.

00:45:57.970 --> 00:45:59.530
Created, called Taffer's Tavern,

00:45:59.530 --> 00:46:02.370
did our franchise registration,
started to franchise.

00:46:02.370 --> 00:46:03.850
COVID happens.

00:46:03.850 --> 00:46:05.890
We find out we don't have any raw protein

00:46:05.890 --> 00:46:06.723
in our restaurant.

00:46:06.723 --> 00:46:08.290
It's all sous vide cooking.

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:09.780
Everything is individually wrapped,

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:11.290
prepared in computerized ovens.

00:46:11.290 --> 00:46:12.820
It's really high quality.

00:46:12.820 --> 00:46:15.380
So we don't have raw, uncooked product.

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:16.440
We don't have grease.

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:18.030
We don't even have hoods.

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:20.380
So we have a very different cooking model.

00:46:20.380 --> 00:46:22.400
It is the kitchen of the future.

00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:25.070
We are the most COVID-safe
kitchen in the world.

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:27.020
We didn't even design it this way.

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:29.370
So now we put uniforms
on our kitchen staff,

00:46:29.370 --> 00:46:30.620
we have cameras in the kitchen

00:46:30.620 --> 00:46:33.210
so you can see your food be
prepared right on your phone,

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:36.470
and we created the safest,
cleanest kitchen concept

00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:39.710
in the country, starting
on a whole different basis.

00:46:39.710 --> 00:46:41.190
So I launched my franchise,

00:46:41.190 --> 00:46:42.350
and I'm answering your question for you,

00:46:42.350 --> 00:46:43.400
'cause this is interesting, Dave,

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:45.360
and we sold a bunch of franchises.

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:47.540
We sold Washington, we sold Atlanta,

00:46:47.540 --> 00:46:48.790
a whole bunch of territories.

00:46:48.790 --> 00:46:49.810
We're doing very, very well.

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:51.084
The franchise is going.

00:46:51.084 --> 00:46:56.084
COVID hits, shoop, sales
stop at franchises.

00:46:56.340 --> 00:46:58.570
About 45 days later we get a phone call

00:46:58.570 --> 00:46:59.910
from some people that say, "Listen,

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:01.340
we're a franchise company.

00:47:01.340 --> 00:47:04.360
We're interested in buying
your franchise and here's why.

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.520
We wanna buy it now because we think

00:47:06.520 --> 00:47:08.060
that some of the greatest
restaurant locations

00:47:08.060 --> 00:47:09.740
in America are become available

00:47:09.740 --> 00:47:11.900
in the next 90 days.

00:47:11.900 --> 00:47:14.410
And we wanna jump on those locations

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:17.880
with a concept that has good,
solid economic modeling."

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.980
And I never quite thought that way.

00:47:20.980 --> 00:47:22.990
So we just sold the city of Boston,

00:47:22.990 --> 00:47:24.990
we just sold the city of Washington DC

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.450
to sophisticated developers who are now

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.520
dropping them in places like Faneuil Hall,

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:31.800
'cause these locations
have become available.

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:33.460
My point is this.

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:35.610
There's opportunity now.

00:47:35.610 --> 00:47:37.510
And the industry is
gonna go through a shift,

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:38.890
there's no question about it.

00:47:38.890 --> 00:47:40.940
But there's great locations that are gonna

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:42.070
be available now.

00:47:42.070 --> 00:47:44.390
There's aggressive
landlords who will give you

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:46.300
very aggressive deals right now

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:48.140
with tenant allowant packages,

00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:49.930
and free rent for a year or two.

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:52.980
I mean, they will bend over
backwards to do those deals.

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:55.110
So to that fictitious restaurant guy

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:56.300
that we were just talking about

00:47:56.300 --> 00:48:00.720
who's scared to do it, maybe
this is the time to do it.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:03.210
And I put a fascinating
thought model out there.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:04.830
Now, we have a big to-go element

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:06.630
that we've put in in Taffer's Tavern

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:09.930
and a curbside element, so
we've shifted the business model

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:11.740
post-COVID, obviously, Dave.

00:48:11.740 --> 00:48:13.080
But my point is this.

00:48:13.080 --> 00:48:15.540
If you've ever wanted to be
in a restaurant business,

00:48:15.540 --> 00:48:17.030
one of the highest costs we got is

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:19.080
occupancy and construction.

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.920
Today, there's unbelievable
opportunity out there.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:24.780
Don't just think that COVID
is bad for the industry.

00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:27.124
It could be good for the industry, too.

00:48:27.124 --> 00:48:29.620
For some people, in the end.

00:48:29.620 --> 00:48:31.280
Interesting view, isn't it?

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:33.210
- Yeah, well, chaos
always brings opportunity.

00:48:33.210 --> 00:48:35.530
I'm actually glad you brought
up the automated kitchen

00:48:35.530 --> 00:48:37.530
and mentioned a little
bit about the economics

00:48:37.530 --> 00:48:39.090
of some of this, 'cause it is something

00:48:39.090 --> 00:48:41.080
that I talk about a lot on the show,

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:43.420
relative to minimum
wage and the rest of it.

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:44.730
So for you, as a guy that want,

00:48:44.730 --> 00:48:46.230
I know you wanna employ people,

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:48.080
you want people to feel empowered,

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:49.330
and feel like they have some control

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:50.670
in their lives and all that,

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:52.590
when you're making those decisions to go,

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:53.423
well, wait a minute.

00:48:53.423 --> 00:48:55.810
I can't pay these people $15 an hour

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:58.170
because technically they're not worth it

00:48:58.170 --> 00:49:00.720
if they don't speak the
language and all that,

00:49:00.720 --> 00:49:03.305
without getting into all
the specifics of that,

00:49:03.305 --> 00:49:05.190
do you have sort of like a,

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:07.280
almost like a philosophical struggle there

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:09.720
between how to go ahead
and do a new business

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:13.330
that isn't gonna hire as
many people, potentially,

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:16.330
but is gonna be more of, say,
a restaurant of the future?

00:49:16.330 --> 00:49:18.210
And something that's sustainable.

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:19.970
That's your whole point.

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:21.023
- Honestly, no.

00:49:22.060 --> 00:49:24.040
And of course there's a personal factor.

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:25.760
You wanna hire as many people as you can.

00:49:25.760 --> 00:49:28.750
My proudest day is when I
had about 900 employees.

00:49:28.750 --> 00:49:30.620
And I used to think to myself every night,

00:49:30.620 --> 00:49:32.670
I put about 1,000 meals
on the table every day.

00:49:32.670 --> 00:49:34.200
I was really proud of that.

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:36.850
But today, my responsibility
is to sustain the jobs

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:38.680
of the people that work for me.

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:41.020
To provide them with an
opportunity that is consistent,

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:43.820
it's gonna last and provide
them with a good income.

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:46.430
Bigger doesn't necessarily
mean more stable.

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:48.320
Doesn't mean more income for everyone.

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:50.990
Also, what about the guy
who built all the robotics?

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:52.340
Who programmed all the systems?

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:54.260
They got jobs that didn't have 'em before.

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:56.250
So I don't think I'm eliminating jobs.

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:58.480
I think I'm redistributing
those jobs a little bit

00:49:58.480 --> 00:50:01.900
into technology plays rater
than just straight cook lines.

00:50:01.900 --> 00:50:05.010
But I think that the restaurant industry

00:50:05.010 --> 00:50:07.140
is being challenged unlike others.

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:09.410
There's many states where tipped employees

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:12.010
get paid about $2.20 an hour.

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:14.480
And it's an employee tip credit of,

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:18.210
whatever it is, $13 an hour,
to adjust their payroll down.

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:20.093
So these employees typically make 20,

00:50:20.093 --> 00:50:23.150
30, $40 an hour in tips
in many of these places.

00:50:23.150 --> 00:50:25.020
So they make $2.20 an hour in payroll.

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:30.020
From $2.20 an hour to $15
is a 600% payroll increase.

00:50:31.420 --> 00:50:33.050
There is no business in the world

00:50:33.050 --> 00:50:36.860
that can sustain a 600% payroll increase.

00:50:36.860 --> 00:50:39.220
So I'm trying to protect my industry.

00:50:39.220 --> 00:50:40.960
I'm trying to protect the people that work

00:50:40.960 --> 00:50:44.113
in the industry by creating
a stable economic model.

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:47.140
There is no guilt in stability.

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:50.050
There is no guilt in
following economic models

00:50:50.050 --> 00:50:53.420
that provide prosperity
for those who work for us.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:54.490
It isn't my choice.

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:56.140
I'd much rather be hiring more people.

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:58.720
But it isn't something
I have any guilt about.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:00.100
- Yeah, I love the answer.

00:51:00.100 --> 00:51:01.830
I mean, that's the honest answer.

00:51:01.830 --> 00:51:03.190
And unfortunately, we've got a lot

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:04.840
of business owners these
days that would give you

00:51:04.840 --> 00:51:07.770
the guilt answer to just
sort of pretend they're

00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:09.030
doing the right thing as opposed

00:51:09.030 --> 00:51:11.763
to actually doing the right thing.

00:51:11.763 --> 00:51:14.700
If somebody's watching this right now

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:16.900
and they're young and they always wanted

00:51:16.900 --> 00:51:19.110
to get into the biz,

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:20.880
you talked about a long resume

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:22.750
of doing basically everything.

00:51:22.750 --> 00:51:23.750
What would you recommend?

00:51:23.750 --> 00:51:26.760
I mean, is the first job
just to be a barback?

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:28.940
Or do you have, like, a specific idea

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:30.920
on how they get in on this?

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:33.200
- Yeah, I think the
most important two jobs

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:34.920
are a line cook and a barback.

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:36.680
You learn everything
from those two positions.

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:37.670
Or bartender.

00:51:37.670 --> 00:51:40.840
So I'm of the belief
that you should not go

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:42.950
into this business until
you at least tend bar

00:51:42.950 --> 00:51:44.660
and work in the kitchen.

00:51:44.660 --> 00:51:46.430
Those are the two areas
where waste occurs,

00:51:46.430 --> 00:51:48.380
those are the two areas
where theft occurs,

00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:50.560
those are the two hearts of the business.

00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:52.880
If it breaks down there,
in either of those places,

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:54.950
you have no business.

00:51:54.950 --> 00:51:57.240
The worst thing in the world
in the restaurant industry

00:51:57.240 --> 00:52:00.270
is to be held hostage to ignorance.

00:52:00.270 --> 00:52:02.090
I can't tell you how many friends I've had

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:03.670
over the years that
have opened restaurants

00:52:03.670 --> 00:52:05.040
that have never been in a business before,

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:08.490
they hire a chef, they pay him
x amount of dollars a year,

00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:10.010
they're open 60 days, the chef says,

00:52:10.010 --> 00:52:13.135
this is all on me, I want
equity or I'm leaving,

00:52:13.135 --> 00:52:14.980
and the guy who owns it doesn't
know how to cook a steak

00:52:14.980 --> 00:52:17.000
or doesn't even know
one of his own recipes.

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:19.850
He's held hostage by his ignorance.

00:52:19.850 --> 00:52:22.210
This is a challenging business.

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:24.240
Think of a retail store,

00:52:24.240 --> 00:52:26.570
but the T-shirts go bad after three days.

00:52:26.570 --> 00:52:27.690
How 'bout that?

00:52:27.690 --> 00:52:29.970
Imagine a T-shirt shop that they rot away

00:52:29.970 --> 00:52:31.423
after three or four days.

00:52:32.790 --> 00:52:33.910
There's theft.

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.100
So we have to manage production,

00:52:36.100 --> 00:52:38.500
we have to manage retail,
manage merchandising,

00:52:38.500 --> 00:52:40.190
manage staff, manage purchasing.

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:42.610
It's a very difficult business.

00:52:42.610 --> 00:52:44.900
And you can't do it from
a point of ignorance.

00:52:44.900 --> 00:52:45.920
Those are the people who fail.

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:46.753
And it's interesting, Dave,

00:52:46.753 --> 00:52:48.280
'cause if you look at the statistics,

00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:50.790
about one in eight restaurants succeed

00:52:50.790 --> 00:52:52.770
for a first-time operator.

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:56.240
Seven of eight succeed
for the second time.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:58.860
- Wow.
- It's a fascinating shift

00:52:58.860 --> 00:52:59.930
to the statistics.

00:52:59.930 --> 00:53:02.240
So once you made those
mistakes the first time,

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:03.990
Dave, you're sorta ready now.

00:53:03.990 --> 00:53:05.830
But without the experience,

00:53:05.830 --> 00:53:07.730
you're not in a very
good statistical place.

00:53:07.730 --> 00:53:08.563
With the experience,

00:53:08.563 --> 00:53:11.070
you're in a far better statistical place.

00:53:11.070 --> 00:53:13.920
